  "text": "The main goal of this work is to compare measured line positions, widths and relative strengths with predictions from a recent quantum‑mechanical model. The data for the two most prominent doublets of the light element were found to agree with the theoretical values when a uniform electric field of roughly one hundred volts per centimetre is assumed. After separating the fine‑structure components and calculating the central line using the relativistic correction theory, the locations of the split parts were obtained from the orbital‑quantization model, while their intensity ratios came from the same quantum framework. All observed frequencies fit the relativistic formula for the series. From this fit a fundamental spectral constant for the element was derived as 1.09677 × 10^5 with an uncertainty of 0.2. The paper also reviews high‑pressure experiments on mixtures of the light element and a noble gas, confirming the consistency of the findings."
